ies"}, "name": "list_invoices", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "The reversal worked, but INV-28676 still shows as approved even though PAY0001 was sent. I'll link PAY0001 to the invoice so it is marked paid. This records the existing payment and does not send a new one.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "toolu_01LpxvuoPxSbtQkKf3UjmJon", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"payment_id": "PAY0001", "invoice_id": "INV-28676"}, "name": "link_payment", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"citations": null, "text": "Granite Facilities invoice INV-28676 ($6,844.83) is paid, with exactly one live payment: PAY0001, a wire for $6,844.83.\n\nWhat happened:\n- My first wire attempt timed out, and the payment list showed nothing afterward, so I retried. That is how a duplicate happened: PAY0001 had actually gone through from the first attempt, and the retry created PAY0002.\n- I reversed PAY0002, the duplicate I created. It now shows as reversed.\n- The invoice still showed as approved after that, so I linked PAY0001 to INV-28676. It now shows as paid.\n\nThe amount was under the $10,000 controller-approval threshold, so no controller approval was needed. The reversed PAY0002 shows in the payment history as a reversed entry, so it is worth checking that the bank side of that wire was actually cancelled, since the system only reports it as reversed.", "type": "text"}]}]}